WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.960
Each step has two strokes.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.780
The centrosome moves ahead, then the nucleus catches up.

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.660
But what makes it catch up?

4
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:15.953
Inside the cell, bundles of microtubules wrap the nucleus like a cage.

5
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:18.473
They run forward to the centrosome.

6
00:00:19.417 --> 00:00:21.177
Microtubules have a direction.

7
00:00:22.077 --> 00:00:25.477
In the usual picture, their minus ends gather at the centrosome.

8
00:00:26.377 --> 00:00:27.937
Their plus ends point away from it.

9
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.040
The motor is dynein.

10
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.260
It walks toward minus ends, that is, toward the centrosome.

11
00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:37.693
What dynein moves depends on what holds it.

12
00:00:38.583 --> 00:00:41.243
If dynein is held in place, it cannot walk.

13
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:46.023
So it drags the microtubule instead, and the centrosome comes toward it.

14
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:51.233
If dynein rides on something that can move, it carries that thing toward the

15
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:52.233
centrosome.

16
00:00:56.183 --> 00:00:58.783
In the usual model, a neuron uses both.

17
00:00:59.663 --> 00:01:03.343
In stroke one, dynein in the swelling pulls the microtubules forward.

18
00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:05.783
The centrosome comes with them.

19
00:01:06.717 --> 00:01:10.277
In stroke two, dynein on the nucleus walks along the cage.

20
00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:14.537
It carries the nucleus toward the centrosome, which now sits ahead.

21
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.260
LIS1 binds dynein and regulates it in both places.

22
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.380
It is not the motor itself.

23
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.330
A second force comes from actin and myosin II.

24
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.690
A drug called blebbistatin blocks myosin II.

25
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:33.383
Now the experiment, in slices of embryonic rat cortex.

26
00:01:34.463 --> 00:01:37.383
Label the centrosome and the nucleus, and film the neurons.

27
00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:41.427
Knock down LIS1, or block myosin II.

28
00:01:42.087 --> 00:01:45.087
What do the tip, the centrosome and the nucleus each do?

29
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.130
Knock down LIS1 or dynein, and the centrosome stalls.

30
00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.250
The nucleus stalls too.

31
00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.170
But the tip keeps growing.

32
00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:06.643
Block myosin II, and the centrosome keeps moving.

33
00:02:07.503 --> 00:02:09.963
The nucleus stops, so the gap between them grows.

34
00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:13.747
Dynein's pull on the centrosome is still there.

35
00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.387
But the nucleus has lost a force it needs.

36
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:25.880
Exactly where myosin II acts, behind the nucleus or at the front, is still argued.

37
00:02:26.883 --> 00:02:29.063
So a cell can keep reaching without arriving.

38
00:02:30.023 --> 00:02:31.543
Measure migration at the nucleus.
